            <div xml:id="d669">

                <pb facs="0792" n="680" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg680"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13022: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-17T18:00:00+08:00">April 17, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 17—9:32 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>157. Legation’s 155, April 16, 10 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >p.m.</hi>].<anchor n="89" xml:id="d669-f1"/> Following from Consul General
                    at Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“April 17, noon. Letter Chengtu April 13 states 29th Szechuan Army ran
                            away and Reds have obtained supplies, guns and ammunition hence their
                            present push. At that time did not believe evacuation Chengtu would be
                            necessary. Two messages from <hi rendition="#italic">Palos</hi> dated
                            April 16th which Legation doubtless received state <hi
                                rendition="#italic">inter alia</hi> Chengtu British [and] American
                            missionaries starting preliminary evacuation <pb facs="0793" n="681"
                                xml:id="pg681"/> women children toward Tzechow and Chungking. Ford
                            plane going Chengtu 17th assist evacuation.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
